# Docker context for `langgraph build` is this directory (langgraph_cli sets the build
# context to langgraph.json's parent, then `ADD`s the lot), so what is listed here is what
# stays out of the deploy image. Nothing else reads this file — local runs are unaffected.
#
# The entries that matter are the two big ones: .chat-ui (the dev-only Next frontend,
# node_modules included) and data/ (20M+ of fetched abstracts, re-fetchable and
# machine-local). .venv too — the image installs its own (scripts/deploy.py says from
# what), and a macOS venv would be wrong in a Linux image even if it were small.
#
# `**/` on anything that can appear at depth: unlike .gitignore, a bare `node_modules/`
# here matches only the context root, so ui/node_modules would otherwise ship.
#
# Do NOT add pyproject.toml, uv.lock, langgraph.json, langgraph.deploy.json, deep_life_sci/,
# ui/, package.json, package-lock.json, models.yaml or README.md: the configs and
# deep_life_sci/ are the build inputs, ui/ and the two npm files are the artifact
# components the graph bundles, the server reads models.yaml at run time, and pyproject
# declares `readme = "README.md"`, so excluding it fails the build outright.
.chat-ui/
# Frontend sources for that same dev-only app; setup copies them into .chat-ui/src.
chat-ui-overlay/
.venv/
*.egg-info/
# A host Node setup unpacked for the chat UI; the image never runs the UI.
.node/
.node.partial/
data/
# No trailing slash: in a git worktree `.git` is a file, not a directory.
.git
.github/
.claude/
**/node_modules/
**/.next/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[oc]
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.langgraph_api/
**/.DS_Store

# Secrets stay on the host. langgraph.json's `env` becomes docker-compose's `env_file`,
# read at run time from outside the image, so the file itself is never needed in here.
# `.env.deploy` is what scripts/deploy.py uploads as deployment secrets, not image content.
.env
.env.*

# Example attachments for trying the composer by hand. 3 MB of PDFs, figures and
# reference exports that nothing at run time reads.
samples/

# Measures the agent rather than being part of it, and nothing shipped should carry a test
# framework (see CLAUDE.md on why evals/ sits outside the package). Safe to drop because
# [tool.setuptools] names the packages explicitly and the version is static — the build
# reads neither evals/ nor .git/.
evals/
# The unit suite, for the same reason.
tests/
